      "source": [
        "### Write-up\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose `get_weather` returned an empty DataFrame on failure instead of raising. Walk through what happens next: you merge it onto 200 days of sales and chart rain against revenue. What does the chart show, and why would nobody catch it?\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "If get_weather returned an empty DataFrame instead of raising, the merge would still succeed, since pandas doesn't treat an empty table as an error. With a left merge, all 200 days of sales stay but every rain value is NaN. With an inner merge, every row is dropped. The chart would then come out blank or show only a few stray points, because plotting libraries skip NaN values without complaint. It can easily be read as a real finding, like \"rain has little effect on revenue,\" when the truth is that the rain data is missing. Nobody would catch it because nothing fails: no exception, no warning, just a chart that renders. Only someone who checks the row or NaN count after the merge, or who knows those days were rainy, would notice. That's why raising an exception is better: it stops the pipeline at the source instead of letting bad data quietly reach a business decision."
      ],
      "id": "EW2iDKeAG8ce"
    },
